Chapter: WirePayloadRefuseTest
  cites Works chapter AnnotationTransport
  cites Works chapter RepoProtocol
  cites Works chapter KeyManager
  cites Works chapter Annotation
  cites Works chapter FactSync
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A verified envelope says who sent the message. It says nothing about whether
 the payload inside arrived whole, and until 2026-08-15 nothing downstream
 asked: a truncated payload decoded to empty fields that a legitimately empty
 one also produces, and the dispatcher acted on it.

 The message below is not corrupt and not forged. It is a correctly signed
 envelope around a SHORT payload, which is what an attacker who can cut a
 stream gets for free and what a half-written frame produces by accident. The
 envelope verifies in both arms -- that is the point, and `envelope=yes` on the
 truncated arm is the line that says so.

 `good` is the acceptance control. A guard that drops everything passes a
 refusal count and fails here.

Section: Fixtures

  kp : Keypair
  kp = generate-keypair [7, 7, 7, 7]

  sample-verdict : Verdict
  sample-verdict = Verdict {
    id = "v1",
    proposal-id = "p1",
    author-fingerprint = kp.fingerprint,
    kind = Accept,
    reason = "looks right",
    signature = [],
    timestamp = 99
  }

  cut : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cut (xs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else cut xs n (i + 1) (acc & [list-at xs i])

 The envelope is rebuilt over the truncated bytes rather than copied from the
 whole message, so the id and the signature are correct FOR WHAT ARRIVED. A
 test that reused the original envelope would be testing the id check, which
 already worked.

  envelope-over : List Integer -> VerdictMsg
  envelope-over (payload) =
    let id = envelope-id payload
    in VerdictMsg {
      id = id,
      from = AgentIdentity { public-key = kp.public-key },
      in-reply-to = [],
      payload = payload,
      signature = sign-content kp (envelope-content id (kp.fingerprint) "verdict")
    }

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Test

 The consumer is driven for real rather than stopped at the decode boundary.
 FactSync's verdict path checks the envelope and then decodes, with no keyring
 in the way, so it is the one consumer a test can reach without standing up a
 trust store -- and `accepted` plus `ss-recv-count` are its own words for
 whether the message counted.

  st0 : SyncState
  st0 = sync-init (disk-empty-in (DiskRegion { dr-base = 0 - 1, dr-limit = 0 })) kp

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let whole = encode-verdict sample-verdict
    in let short = cut whole (list-length whole - 9) 0 []
    in let good = envelope-over whole
    in let bad = envelope-over short
    in act
      print-line-uni ("whole len=" & show (list-length whole) & " short len=" & show (list-length short))
      print-line-uni ("good  envelope=" & yn (verify-verdict-envelope good) & " payload-valid=" & yn ((decode-verdict-checked (good.payload)).valid) & " id=" & (decode-verdict (good.payload)).id)
      print-line-uni ("bad   envelope=" & yn (verify-verdict-envelope bad) & " payload-valid=" & yn ((decode-verdict-checked (bad.payload)).valid) & " id=" & (decode-verdict (bad.payload)).id)
      print-line-uni ("bad kind-would-have-been=" & (when (decode-verdict (bad.payload)).kind is Accept -> "Accept" is Reject -> "Reject" is Amend (t) -> "Amend" is Abstain -> "Abstain"))
      print-line-uni ("consumer good accepted=" & yn ((sync-receive st0 (MsgVerdict good) 1000).accepted) & " bad accepted=" & yn ((sync-receive st0 (MsgVerdict bad) 1000).accepted))
      print-line-uni ("consumer good recv=" & show (((sync-receive st0 (MsgVerdict good) 1000).state).ss-recv-count) & " bad recv=" & show (((sync-receive st0 (MsgVerdict bad) 1000).state).ss-recv-count))
    end
  end
